\section{Separate Finding a Binding from Computing Its Value}

An import may request a declaration, an allocated storage cell,
or a fully initialized value. Those requests need not have the
same dependencies. Consider a small drawing application: the
\texttt{Brushes} module registers a standard brush in its initializer,
and \texttt{Canvas} constructs a default view from that brush.
\texttt{Brushes} also imports the type \texttt{Canvas.Bounds}.
The type dependency is harmless at run time; the eager request
for a brush is a real initialization edge. Giving all import
edges the same meaning can either reject this program or allow
it for the wrong reason.

Assume a language in which function declarations can be bound
before executing any initializer, but invoking one can still
read module storage. Allocating the storage for both modules
does not make either module ready. That distinction matters when
an initializer calls a function from another module which in turn
reads a variable in the first. The variable must either have an
explicit language-defined pre-initialization value or the read
must fail; whatever bits happened to be in storage are not a
language policy.

\section{Classify Cycles by the Demands They Contain}

\Cref{fig:visual-400} shows why a compile-time cycle can coexist
with an acyclic run-time order. Signatures can be collected for
the entire component; for initialization, \texttt{Canvas} waits
on \texttt{Brushes} but not the reverse. If \texttt{Brushes} now
asks for the constructed view during registration, add the
reverse value edge. Topologically sorting the original import
graph misses this semantic distinction.

