If dependencies are explicit, diagnose a strongly connected
component of eager demands and produce a short cycle path with
the value requests labeled. If user code can make arbitrary
calls in initializers, a static graph may be incomplete; a
runtime guard can reject a demand when it occurs. Alternatively,
force authors to declare the eager dependencies and restrict
initializers so their dynamic accesses cannot escape the graph.
The implementation below chooses dynamic failure in a serialized
initialization phase, not a fixed point over side effects.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Execute at Most Once and Publish Only on Success}

\begin{verbatim}
prepare(module):
    module.cells := allocate_unpublished_cells()
    module.declarations := bind_function_addresses()
    module.state := Prepared

ensure_ready(m, stack):
    match m.state:
        Ready: return
        Failed(cause): raise prior_failure(m, cause)
        Running: raise initialization_cycle(stack, m)
        Prepared: pass
    m.state := Running
    stack.push(m)
    try:
        pending := run_initializer(m, stack)
        verify_all_required_values(pending)
        m.cells := pending
        m.state := Ready
    catch exc:
        m.state := Failed(exc)
        raise exc
    finally:
        stack.pop()

read_export(m, name, stack):
    ensure_ready(m, stack)
    return m.cells[name]
\end{verbatim}

Here exported value reads are subject to readiness; an initializer
uses its own staged cells directly under the language's local
definite-assignment rules. Running into itself via an exported
value read fails. A language need not choose this exact behavior.
The JVM specifies four initialization states for classes and
interfaces and treats a same-thread recursive request differently:
it allows that request to complete normally, potentially exposing
the effects of preparation and whatever initialization has run so
far. That behavior should not be confused with reexecuting the
initializer or with a transaction that publishes every write at once.

\section{Make Failure and Ordering Observable on Purpose}

Staging the module's exports does not undo actions on other
objects. If an initializer changes a shared registry and then
raises, a failed state prevents a second attempt but does not
erase the first attempt's registry changes. A production API can
prepare candidate registrations privately, commit once at the
end, and use an explicit compensating operation only where it
can prove the operation is safe. Avoid automatic retry for effects
whose execution count is externally observable.

For independent modules, specify whether initializer order is
observable and whether a deterministic tie-break is part of the
contract. Concurrent initialization needs more than running the
same code on two threads: readiness must publish writes with
appropriate synchronization, failures must wake waiters, and
waiting on another owner introduces cross-thread cycles. A single
initialization executor precludes those particular wait cycles if
initializers cannot create their own blocking waits. Test a
diamond (the common prerequisite runs once), a failing action
(the failure is replayed without rerunning), and a reentrant value
read (the cycle path includes both requests). Log effect events as
well as outputs so a duplicate registration cannot go unnoticed.

\section{Decide Where to Put Delayed Work}

Languages can also put a module's startup order directly into
their specification. D, for example, defines lexical order for
static constructors inside a module and reverse order for static
destruction. This concerns execution of effects, not the order
in which the compiler collects the module's declarations.

One way to fix the brush cycle is to have the registry store a
factory instead of a constructed view. The factory captures enough
information to fetch the default view when invoked. It must not
fetch the view when registered; that would reintroduce the demand
at the old point. This changes the public contract: reading a
registry entry now yields a factory, and forcing it can fail or
produce effects later. The compiler cannot make this rewrite
merely because it found a cycle unless the language treats that
change in timing as unobservable.

\begin{verbatim}
eager startup:
    Canvas asks for Brushes.default
    Brushes asks for Canvas.default_view
    Canvas is Running: cycle

factory startup:
    Canvas asks for Brushes.default
    Brushes publishes the brush and a view factory
    Canvas publishes its default_view
    client calls the factory: Canvas is Ready
\end{verbatim}

If a registry routine immediately invokes a factory to validate it,
the factory version still fails. Document which calls merely store
a callback and which calls execute it. The same issue appears
when a nominally lazy value computes its key eagerly or demands
a dependency to log a description. The initialization trace must
include these reads, not just the textual imports. Unstructured
callbacks in startup code can make the value graph harder to
reason about than the module graph.

An alternative is to extract the common state into a third module
with no initialization dependencies and pass prepared values into
an explicit startup routine. This eliminates the cycle by moving
control to the application rather than delaying it into the
first read. Compare the two options with a fixture that observes
when factory work starts. In an eager setup the failure is part
of startup; in the delayed setup it may happen after drawing has
begun. That choice belongs in the library's initialization contract,
not in an incidental traversal order chosen by the loader.
